<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Millawanda</h1><p class="st-meta">1450 BCE – 1200 BCE (approx.) · capital Miletus</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Bronze Age Miletus as named in Hittite texts, a coastal foothold of the Aegean Ahhiyawa on the Anatolian mainland and a flashpoint with the Hittites.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Excavations at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/miletus">Miletus</a> show a Minoan-style settlement from c. 1900 BCE, followed after c. 1450 BCE by a town with Mycenaean pottery, tombs and customs. Most scholars identify this town with Millawanda, or Milawata, named in Hittite sources as a place under the influence of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ahhiyawa">Ahhiyawa</a>, the power of the Mycenaean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tawagalawa-letter">Tawagalawa letter</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusili-iii">Hattusili III</a>, c. 1250 BCE, names Atpa as the local ruler, son-in-law of the rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piyamaradu">Piyamaradu</a>, who used Millawanda as a base for raids on Hittite lands. The town was a port linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> with western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, and the Hittite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mursili-ii">Mursili II</a> had sacked it c. 1315 BCE during his wars with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/arzawa">Arzawa</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The so-called Milawata letter, probably from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tudhaliya-iv">Tudhaliya IV</a>, shows the town had passed under Hittite control and a new border was being fixed. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/miletus">Miletus</a> was destroyed c. 1200 BCE, then resettled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionian Greeks</a> as the city-state Miletus.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/millawanda.jpg" alt="Millawanda" width="320" height="215"><figcaption>Image: Bernard Gagnon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Miletus_-_Ancient_Greek_theatre_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1450 BCE – 1200 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century-bce">15th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century-bce">13th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Miletus</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Atpa</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mycenaean Greek; Luwian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Milawata, Bronze Age Miletus</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-1325&amp;focus=millawanda" aria-label="Millawanda on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5362 827 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Millawanda lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5682 1083L5852 1002"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5852 1002h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5682 1083h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5682 1083h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-1325&amp;focus=millawanda">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

